===Mexico vs Cameroon===
{{further|List of 2014 FIFA World Cup controversies#Mexico vs Cameroon}}
The two teams had met in one previous match, in a friendly in 1993, won by Mexico 1–0.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/51/97/38/2014fwc_kit_matches_neutral.pdf#page=8|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140519222312/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/51/97/38/2014fwc_kit_matches_neutral.pdf#page=8|url-status=dead|archive-date=19 May 2014|title=2014 FIFA World Cup – Statistical Kit|publisher=FIFA.com|page=8}}</ref>

Mexico's [[Giovani dos Santos]] had two goals disallowed for offside in the first half.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/13/mexico-cameroon-world-cup-group-a-match-report|title=Mexico's Peralta finally sees off Cameroon after referee fluffs his lines|date=13 June 2014 |work=The Guardian|access-date=30 October 2014}}</ref>
However, replays showed that he was in line with the last defender for the first case, while the ball came from a Cameroon player for the second.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://metro.co.uk/2014/06/13/world-cup-2014-mexicos-giovani-dos-santos-has-two-goals-wrongly-ruled-out-for-offside-in-first-half-against-cameroon-4760966/ |title=World Cup 2014: Mexico's Giovani Dos Santos has TWO goals WRONGLY ruled out for offside in first-half against Cameroon |publisher=Metro |date=13 June 2014 |access-date=15 June 2014}}</ref> In the second half, dos Santos had a shot saved by the Cameroon goalkeeper, [[Charles Itandje]], but the ball rebounded into the path of [[Oribe Peralta]], who slotted the ball into the empty net with his left foot.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285036 |title=World Cup: Mexico 1–0 Cameroon |publisher=BBC Sport |date=13 June 2014 |access-date=13 June 2014}}</ref>

Mexico captain [[Rafael Márquez]] made history by becoming the first player to captain his nation in four different World Cups.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Keh|first1=Andrew|title=Despite a Wayward Journey, Márquez Is a Rock for Mexico|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/06/17/sports/worldcup/world-cup-2014-rafael-marquez-sets-mark-as-mexicos-captain.html|access-date=1 July 2014|work=The New York Times|date=16 June 2014}}</ref> [[Samuel Eto'o]] also appeared in his fourth World Cup, joining fellow Cameroonians [[Jacques Songo'o]] and [[Rigobert Song]] as the only African players to have achieved the feat.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/23/samuel-etoo-cameroon-world-cup-2018|title=Samuel Eto'o could play at 2018 World Cup, says Cameroon coach|work=The Guardian|date=23 June 2014}}</ref>

