===Croatia vs Mexico===
{{further|List of 2014 FIFA World Cup controversies#Croatia vs Mexico}}
The two teams had met in three previous matches, including in the [[2002 FIFA World Cup]] group stage, won by Mexico [[2002 FIFA World Cup Group G#Croatia vs Mexico|1–0]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/51/97/38/2014fwc_kit_matches_neutral.pdf#page=40|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140519222312/http://www.fifa.com/mm/document/fifafacts/mencompwc/51/97/38/2014fwc_kit_matches_neutral.pdf#page=40|url-status=dead|archive-date=19 May 2014|title=2014 FIFA World Cup – Statistical Kit|publisher=FIFA.com|page=40}}</ref>

Coming into the final round of matches, Croatia needed to win to guarantee qualification to the knockout stage regardless of the result of Cameroon vs Brazil, while Mexico only needed a draw to do so. The game was goalless for the first 70 minutes, until a [[Héctor Herrera]] corner to the back post allowed [[Rafael Márquez]] to score from a header, outleaping the Croatian defenders.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/23/croatia-mexico-world-cup-2014-match-report|title=Mexico qualify from Group A with three late goals against Croatia|date=23 June 2014|work=The Guardian|access-date=24 June 2014}}</ref> With Croatia progressing only by way of a win, they were caught out on a break a short time later where [[Andrés Guardado]] scored after receiving a pass from [[Oribe Peralta]]. Mexico scored their third goal when Márquez flicked on Guardado's corner with substitute [[Javier Hernández]] scoring at the back post. [[Ivan Perišić]] scored in his second consecutive World Cup match after a neat back pass from [[Ivan Rakitić]] to get a consolation goal for Croatia in the closing minutes of the game, before Croatia's [[Ante Rebić]] was sent off for a foul on [[Carlos Alberto Peña|Carlos Peña]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/0/football/25285326|title=Croatia 1 Mexico 3|date=23 June 2014|work=BBC Sport|access-date=24 June 2014}}</ref> Mexico qualified as group runners-up (behind Brazil on goal difference) on virtue of the win, while Croatia were eliminated.

With his goal, Márquez joined [[Cuauhtémoc Blanco]] as the only Mexican player to score in three World Cups.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2107456-why-rafael-marquez-will-be-key-for-mexico-in-the-clash-against-netherlands|title=Why Rafael Marquez Will Be Key for Mexico in the Clash Against Netherlands|publisher=Bleacher Report|date=23 June 2014}}</ref>

